(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-lab-interpretation-hematology-quiz"]={"title":"Lab Interpretation 1: Complete Blood Count — PDM I Exam 1","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has six months of fatigue and heavy periods. Her results are hemoglobin 9.1 g/dL (12.0–15.5) · mean corpuscular volume 72 fL (80–100) · ferritin 6 ng/mL (15–200) · total iron-binding capacity 465 µg/dL (250–400). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Iron deficiency anemia","Anemia of chronic disease","Beta thalassemia trait","Vitamin B12 deficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Microcytic with a LOW ferritin and a HIGH iron-binding capacity — the marrow is short of iron and the body has increased its carrying capacity to hunt for more. In a menstruating woman this is the commonest anemia there is, and it still obliges you to think about occult blood loss. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, iron studies"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with rheumatoid arthritis has mild fatigue. His results are hemoglobin 10.4 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · mean corpuscular volume 84 fL (80–100) · ferritin 410 ng/mL (15–200) · total iron-binding capacity 198 µg/dL (250–400). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Anemia of chronic disease","Iron deficiency anemia","Sideroblastic anemia","Folate deficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ferritin HIGH and binding capacity LOW is the mirror image of iron deficiency: iron exists but inflammation sequesters it, and ferritin rises as an acute phase reactant. The volume sits normocytic or mildly microcytic. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, iron studies"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man of Mediterranean descent is anemic on a routine panel and feels well. His results are hemoglobin 11.8 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · mean corpuscular volume 68 fL (80–100) · red blood cell count 6.1 million/µL (4.5–5.9) · ferritin 142 ng/mL (15–200). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Beta thalassemia trait","Iron deficiency anemia","Anemia of chronic disease","Lead poisoning"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Markedly microcytic with NORMAL iron stores and a red cell count that is high rather than low — the marrow makes plenty of small cells. Iron deficiency cannot produce that combination. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, microcytic causes"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has fatigue and pins and needles in both feet. His results are hemoglobin 9.6 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · mean corpuscular volume 118 fL (80–100) · vitamin B12 94 pg/mL (200–900) · smear macroovalocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils (—). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Folate deficiency","Alcoholic liver disease","Myelodysplastic syndrome","Vitamin B12 deficiency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Macrocytic with a low B12, and the smear gives the megaloblastic signature — macroovalocytes with hypersegmented neutrophils. The neuropathy is what distinguishes it from folate deficiency clinically. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, macrocytic causes"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man who drinks heavily is anemic. His results are hemoglobin 11.2 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · mean corpuscular volume 106 fL (80–100) · vitamin B12 and folate both normal (—) · smear round macrocytes, no hypersegmentation (—). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Non-megaloblastic macrocytosis of liver disease and alcohol","Folate deficiency","Hereditary spherocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Macrocytic with normal B12 and folate and, decisively, NO macroovalocytes or hypersegmented neutrophils. Without those the problem is not DNA synthesis, and alcohol and liver disease are the usual explanation. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, macrocytic causes"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman is admitted after a road traffic collision with a pelvic fracture. Two days later her results are hemoglobin 8.4 g/dL (12.0–15.5) · mean corpuscular volume 89 fL (80–100) · reticulocyte count 6.8% (0.5–2.5) · ferritin 165 ng/mL (15–200). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Iron deficiency anemia","Acute blood loss anemia","Aplastic anemia","Anemia of chronic disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Normocytic with a HIGH reticulocyte count — the marrow is responding normally to a sudden loss, and the cells it makes are of normal size. Stores are still adequate, which is why ferritin is untouched this early. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, normocytic branch"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has jaundice and dark urine. Her results are hemoglobin 7.9 g/dL (12.0–15.5) · reticulocyte count 9.2% (0.5–2.5) · unconjugated bilirubin 4.1 mg/dL (0.2–0.8) · haptoglobin undetectable (30–200 mg/dL). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute blood loss anemia","Hemolytic anemia","Gilbert syndrome","Aplastic anemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A high reticulocyte count with a raised UNCONJUGATED bilirubin and an undetectable haptoglobin is the hemolysis triad — cells are being destroyed, free hemoglobin is mopping up the haptoglobin, and the marrow is compensating hard. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, hemolysis"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has fatigue, bruising and recurrent infections. His results are hemoglobin 7.2 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · white blood cell count 1,900/µL (4,500–11,000) · platelets 24,000/µL (150,000–400,000) · reticulocyte count 0.2% (0.5–2.5). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Immune thrombocytopenic purpura","Aplastic anemia","Hemolytic anemia","Iron deficiency anemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All three lines are down and the reticulocyte count is LOW — the marrow is not producing anything. That combination of pancytopenia with an absent reticulocyte response is what defines marrow failure. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, pancytopenia"},{"q":"A 4-year-old living in old housing has irritability and abdominal pain. Her results are hemoglobin 9.8 g/dL (11.5–15.5) · mean corpuscular volume 70 fL (80–100) · ferritin 88 ng/mL (15–200) · smear coarse basophilic stippling (—). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Iron deficiency anemia","Beta thalassemia trait","Anemia of chronic disease","Lead poisoning"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Microcytic with NORMAL iron stores, and the smear finding settles it — basophilic stippling is ribosomal RNA scattered evenly through the cytoplasm, and the classic association is lead. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, red cell inclusions"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has anemia and splenomegaly. His results are hemoglobin 10.1 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · mean corpuscular volume 82 fL (80–100) · mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration 38 g/dL (32–36) · smear round cells lacking central pallor (—). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Iron deficiency anemia","Sickle cell disease","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Hereditary spherocytosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A RAISED mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration is the automated flag for it, and the smear matches — perfectly round cells that have lost their central pallor. Few things push that index above the range. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man had a splenectomy after trauma five years ago. His routine smear shows single dark purple nuclear fragments in red cells present (absent) · target cells present (absent) · hemoglobin 14.1 g/dL (13.5–17.5). What is the most likely cause?","choices":["Iron deficiency has produced the inclusions","He has lead poisoning","He has no spleen to remove the inclusions","He has glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those fragments are Howell-Jolly bodies, and the spleen normally plucks them out — so finding them means absent or non-functioning splenic tissue. Target cells appear after splenectomy too, from redundant membrane. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Howell-Jolly bodies"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man develops anemia days after starting an oxidant drug. His results are hemoglobin 8.8 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · reticulocyte count 7.1% (0.5–2.5) · supravital stain inclusions at the cell periphery (—) · smear bite cells (—). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hereditary spherocytosis","Immune hemolytic anemia","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","Lead poisoning"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heinz bodies are denatured hemoglobin sitting at the edge of the cell and are invisible without a supravital stain; the spleen bites them out, leaving bite cells. An oxidant trigger completes the picture. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Heinz bodies"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has a fever and a productive cough. Her results are white blood cell count 19,400/µL (4,500–11,000) · neutrophils 84% (54–62) · bands 11% (0–5) · lymphocytes 9% (24–44). What is the most likely cause?","choices":["A viral infection","A parasitic infestation","A bacterial infection","An allergic reaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils dominate and bands are raised — a left shift, meaning the marrow is releasing immature neutrophils faster than it can mature them. That pairing is the bacterial signature. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, neutrophils and left shift"},{"q":"A 9-year-old has a week of fever, sore throat and fatigue. His results are white blood cell count 14,800/µL (4,500–11,000) · lymphocytes 62% (24–44) · neutrophils 28% (54–62) · smear atypical lymphocytes (—). What is the most likely cause?","choices":["A bacterial infection","A viral infection","An allergic reaction","A bone marrow failure syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A lymphocyte-predominant leukocytosis with atypical lymphocytes on the smear is the viral pattern, and in this age group with pharyngitis it points hard at infectious mononucleosis. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, lymphocytes"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man recently back from travel has itching and a rash. His results are white blood cell count 12,100/µL (4,500–11,000) · eosinophils 18% (1–3) · neutrophils 48% (54–62) · hemoglobin 13.9 g/dL (13.5–17.5). What is the most likely cause?","choices":["A parasitic infestation or an allergic process","A bacterial infection","A viral infection","Corticosteroid therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Eosinophils are the line raised by parasites, allergy and — less often — malignancy. At 18% against a range of 1 to 3, the eosinophil is the whole finding here. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, eosinophils"},{"q":"A patient on chemotherapy is reviewed before the next cycle. His results are white blood cell count 1,200/µL (4,500–11,000) · neutrophils 22% (54–62) · bands 6% (0–5). Calculate the absolute neutrophil count and grade it.","choices":["336 per microlitre — severe neutropenia","264 per microlitre — severe neutropenia","336 per microlitre — moderate neutropenia","1,200 per microlitre — mild neutropenia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bands count WITH the neutrophils: 1,200 × (22 + 6) ÷ 100 = 336. Below 500 is severe, which is the threshold that changes precautions and antibiotic decisions. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, absolute neutrophil count"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has petechiae and gum bleeding after a viral illness. She is otherwise well. Her results are platelets 18,000/µL (150,000–400,000) · hemoglobin 13.4 g/dL (12.0–15.5) · white blood cell count 7,100/µL (4,500–11,000) · smear no schistocytes (—). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Immune thrombocytopenic purpura","Aplastic anemia","Disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An ISOLATED thrombocytopenia — red cells and white cells untouched — with a normal smear after a viral illness. The absence of schistocytes is what separates it from the microangiopathies. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, platelets"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has confusion, fever and bruising. Her results are platelets 22,000/µL (150,000–400,000) · hemoglobin 8.1 g/dL (12.0–15.5) · reticulocyte count 6.4% (0.5–2.5) · smear numerous schistocytes (—). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Immune thrombocytopenic purpura","Iron deficiency anemia","A thrombotic microangiopathy","Hereditary spherocytosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Low platelets PLUS a hemolytic anemia with schistocytes is consumption inside small vessels — red cells are being sheared as they pass. The fever and confusion complete the classic pentad. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, schistocytes"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has headaches, itching after hot showers and a ruddy face. He does not smoke. His results are hemoglobin 19.8 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · hematocrit 59% (41–53) · white blood cell count 14,200/µL (4,500–11,000) · platelets 612,000/µL (150,000–400,000). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Polycythemia vera","Chronic hypoxia from lung disease","Dehydration","Anemia of chronic disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All THREE lines are raised, which points at a clonal marrow disorder rather than a response to anything. A secondary cause drives red cells alone; it has no reason to raise white cells and platelets too. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, red cell count raised"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with cirrhosis has a routine panel. His results are hemoglobin 11.6 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · mean corpuscular volume 103 fL (80–100) · smear cells with a dark centre inside the pale area (—) · vitamin B12 and folate normal (—). What is the most likely cause?","choices":["Vitamin B12 deficiency has impaired DNA synthesis","Iron is being lost chronically from the gut","The spleen is sequestering red cells","Liver disease has added redundant membrane to the red cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those are target cells — a bullseye produced by too much membrane for the volume of hemoglobin. Liver disease and splenectomy are the two classic causes, and the macrocytosis here is non-megaloblastic. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, target cells"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has anemia and a firm enlarged spleen. His results are hemoglobin 9.2 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · smear teardrop-shaped red cells (—) · platelets 96,000/µL (150,000–400,000) · white blood cell count 3,800/µL (4,500–11,000). What is the most likely cause?","choices":["The spleen has been removed","Oxidant stress is denaturing hemoglobin","The marrow is being infiltrated and replaced","Iron stores are exhausted"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Teardrop cells are made when red cells squeeze out of a marrow packed with scar tissue or tumour. With cytopenias in all three lines and splenomegaly, infiltration is the mechanism. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, teardrop cells"},{"q":"A results screen shows a value that does not look right to the clinician. The results are hemoglobin 9.0 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · hematocrit 48% (41–53). Why is this pair implausible?","choices":["The hematocrit should equal the hemoglobin","The hemoglobin should be roughly three times the hematocrit","Both values are normal, so nothing is wrong","The hematocrit should be roughly three times the hemoglobin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Multiply the hemoglobin by three and the hematocrit should land nearby — 9 predicts about 27, not 48. A pair that far apart means a sampling or analyser problem worth repeating. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, hemoglobin against hematocrit"},{"q":"A panel reports the indices below. The results are mean corpuscular volume 74 fL (80–100) · mean corpuscular hemoglobin 23 pg (27–33) · mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration 29 g/dL (32–36). How are these cells best described?","choices":["Macrocytic and normochromic","Normocytic and hyperchromic","Microcytic and hypochromic","Microcytic and hyperchromic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Volume below range makes them small, and both hemoglobin indices below range make them pale — central pallor exceeding a third of the diameter. Iron deficiency and thalassemia sit here. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, red cell indices"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has a microcytic anemia. Her results are mean corpuscular volume 76 fL (80–100) · ferritin 120 ng/mL (15–200) · serum iron 82 µg/dL (60–170) · total iron-binding capacity 310 µg/dL (250–400) · smear no stippling (—). What does this pattern point to?","choices":["Iron deficiency anemia","Lead poisoning","Anemia of chronic disease","Thalassemia trait"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Microcytic with all three iron studies normal and no stippling — the algorithm's endpoint. Iron is available, so the small cells are a production defect rather than a supply problem. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, microcytic algorithm"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman on prednisone for polymyalgia has a raised white count and feels well, with no fever. Her results are white blood cell count 14,600/µL (4,500–11,000) · neutrophils 82% (54–62) · bands 2% (0–5) · eosinophils 0% (1–3). What is the most likely cause?","choices":["An occult bacterial infection","Corticosteroids have demarginated neutrophils","A myeloproliferative disorder","An allergic reaction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Steroids detach neutrophils from the vessel wall into the circulating pool, so the count rises without any new production — which is why bands stay normal. They also suppress eosinophils, and here eosinophils are zero. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, demargination"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman with heavy periods has these results hemoglobin 9.4 g/dL (12.0–15.5) · mean corpuscular volume 71 fL (80–100) · platelets 580,000/µL (150,000–400,000) · ferritin 7 ng/mL (15–200). How should the platelet count be read?","choices":["Evidence of a second, independent bone marrow disorder","A reactive rise that iron deficiency itself causes","A laboratory artefact requiring a repeat sample","A sign of impending bleeding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Iron deficiency is one of the listed causes of a raised platelet count, alongside trauma, acute hemorrhage and polycythemia vera. It needs no separate workup — treat the iron deficiency and it settles. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, platelets raised"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has back pain and anemia. His results are hemoglobin 9.7 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · smear red cells stacked in chains like rows of coins (—) · total protein 10.8 g/dL (6.0–8.3) · erythrocyte sedimentation rate 112 mm/hr (0–20). What is the most likely cause?","choices":["Antibodies are agglutinating the red cells","The marrow is infiltrated by fibrous tissue","Raised serum proteins have neutralised the cells' surface charge","The cells have lost membrane and become spherical"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That stacking is rouleaux: red cells normally repel each other with a negative surface charge, and excess protein cancels it so they clump in chains. The very high total protein and sedimentation rate fit a paraprotein. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, rouleaux"},{"q":"A well 30-year-old man has a pre-employment panel. His results are hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · mean corpuscular volume 91 fL (80–100) · white blood cell count 7,600/µL (4,500–11,000) · platelets 268,000/µL (150,000–400,000). How should this panel be reported?","choices":["Normal, with no further testing indicated","Early iron deficiency, because the volume is below the midpoint","Mild anemia, because the hemoglobin is not at the top of the range","Thrombocytopenia, because the platelet count is under 300,000"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every value sits inside its range. Recognising a normal panel is a real skill — a reference range is the mean plus or minus two standard deviations, so chasing values that are merely near an edge generates work rather than answers. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, reference ranges"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is found to have iron deficiency anemia on a routine panel. He reports no bleeding. His results are hemoglobin 10.2 g/dL (13.5–17.5) · mean corpuscular volume 70 fL (80–100) · ferritin 5 ng/mL (15–200). What must accompany treatment?","choices":["Nothing beyond oral iron and a repeat panel","Bone marrow biopsy","Hemoglobin electrophoresis","Evaluation for occult blood loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a man of this age iron deficiency is a bleeding diagnosis until proven otherwise, and it is often the first sign of gastrointestinal bleeding. Replacing the iron without finding the leak treats the number, not the patient. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, iron deficiency"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has fatigue and early satiety with a very large spleen. His results are white blood cell count 96,000/µL (4,500–11,000) · differential the full range of maturing granulocytes (—) · platelets 540,000/µL (150,000–400,000) · hemoglobin 10.8 g/dL (13.5–17.5). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A myeloproliferative disorder","A bacterial infection","A viral infection","Corticosteroid therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A white count near 100,000 with granulocytes at every stage of maturation and a massive spleen is clonal overproduction, not a response to infection. The raised platelets and mild anemia fit the same process. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, white cell count raised"}]};
